  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 239 AM MST Fri Nov 13 2026\n\nEarly morning satellite imagery shows mid-to-high level clouds \nstreaming over eastern Idaho as the upper-level ridge \nresponsible for our recent stretch of pleasant, above-normal \nweather begins to break down. We will experience one final \nseasonably mild day today with highs reaching the 50s and 60s. \nBeyond today, however, temperatures in the 60s will depart for \nthe foreseeable future as a much cooler and wetter regime \nsettles across the region.\n\nThe first round of rain and snow sweeps through quickly tonight\ninto Saturday alongside split upper-level flow across Idaho. \nThis split trajectory, paired with southerly low-level flow, \nwill generate sharp terrain-driven precipitation contrasts. \nDownsloping off the southern highlands will initially limit \nprecipitation across portions of the Snake River Plain, Magic \nValley, and adjacent bench areas, leaving several valley \nlocations rain-shadowed with totals ranging from a couple of \nhundredths up to 0.20 inches. Conversely, southerly flow will \nbank moisture against the northern rim of the Plain, creating \nfavorable upslope enhancement into the Wood River Valley and \nsurrounding ranges. Expect 0.20 to 0.40 inches of liquid \nequivalent across the Wood River Valley and adjacent mountains, \nand up to 0.30 inches from around Pomerelle eastward through the\nBear River Range, though some spread remains regarding the \nexact areal coverage of those higher amounts. Snowfall will \nremain generally above 7,000 feet, keeping impactful \naccumulations confined above pass level at 1 to 3 inches, with \nlocalized upslope totals up to 5 inches across parts of the \nSawtooths, Boulders, and Pioneers. Daytime highs on Saturday \nwill drop back into the 30s and 40s areawide.\n\nEastern Idaho will reside between departing and incoming upper-\nlevel troughs on Sunday. Dry conditions will return under partly\ncloudy skies, allowing afternoon temperatures to rebound by\naround 5 degrees compared to Saturday's readings. The next\nPacific storm shifts slowly inland on Monday. Even as this low\nsplits while moving eastward, it will deliver another round of\nrain and mountain snow focused primarily across the Central\nMountains back into south-central and southwest Idaho. Looking\nfurther ahead, global guidance indicates a large, slow-moving\nupper trough carving out across the western United States during\nthe middle to latter half of next week. Temperatures will trend\nsteadily downward throughout the week ahead, with valley highs\nlikely restricted to the upper 30s and lower 40s by the end of\nthe forecast period \u2014 cold enough for snow to become the\ndominant precipitation type regionwide beyond mid-week.",
